example.paragraph:
Let $A$ be the matrix in (2), and let $\mathbf{u} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      5 \\
      3 \\
      {- 2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. Determine if $\mathbf{u}$ belongs to the null space of $A$.

The first step is to find the general solution of $A\textbf{x} = 0$ in terms of free variables. Row reduce the augmented matrix $\begin{bmatrix}
      A & 0
      \end{bmatrix}$ to reduced echelon form in order to write the basic variables in terms of the free variables:

paragraph.math:
$$\begin{array}{ll}
      \left\lbrack \begin{array}{l}
      x_{1} \\
      x_{2} \\
      x_{3} \\
      x_{4} \\
      x_{5}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack & {= \left\lbrack \begin{array}{l}
      {2x_{2} + x_{4} - 3x_{5}} \\
      x_{2} \\
      {- 2x_{4} + 2x_{5}} \\
      x_{4} \\
      x_{5}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack = x_{2\,}\underset{\begin{array}{l}
       \uparrow \\
      \textbf{u}
      \end{array}}{\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      2 \\
      1 \\
      0 \\
      0 \\
      0
      \end{array} \right\rbrack} + x_{4\,}\underset{\begin{array}{l}
       \uparrow \\
      \textbf{v}
      \end{array}}{\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      0 \\
      {- 2} \\
      1 \\
      0
      \end{array} \right\rbrack} + x_{5}\underset{\begin{array}{l}
       \uparrow \\
      \textbf{w}
      \end{array}}{\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 3} \\
      0 \\
      2 \\
      0 \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack}} \\
       & {= x_{2}\textbf{u} + x_{4}\textbf{v} + x_{5}\textbf{w}}
      \end{array}$$

note.paragraph:
The column space of an $m \times n$ matrix $A$, written as Col $A$, is the set of all linear combinations of the columns of $A$. If $A = \begin{bmatrix}
      \mathbf{a}_{1} & \ldots & \mathbf{a}_{n}
      \end{bmatrix}$, then

paragraph.math:
$$\begin{matrix}
      {M_{2}\,\mspace{-4mu}\left( \left\{ p_{k} \right\} + \left\{ q_{k} \right\} \right) = M_{2}\,\mspace{-4mu}\left( \left\{ p_{k} + q_{k} \right\} \right)} & = & \left\{ \frac{p_{k} + q_{k} + p_{k - 1} + q_{k - 1}}{2} \right\} \\
       & = & {\left\{ \frac{p_{k} + p_{k - 1}}{2} \right\}\, + \,\,\left\{ \frac{q_{k} + q_{k - 1}}{2} \right\}} \\
       & = & {M_{2}\left( \left\{ p_{k} \right\} \right)~ + ~M_{2}\left( \left\{ q_{k} \right\} \right)}
      \end{matrix}$$

statement.paragraph:
Determine if $\textbf{w} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      3 \\
      {- 4}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ is in Nul $A$, where

paragraph.math:
$\left\{ \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      a \\
      b \\
      c \\
      d
      \end{array} \right\rbrack:\begin{array}{l}
      {a + 3b = c} \\
      {b + c + a = d}
      \end{array} \right\}$

statement.paragraph:
Let $A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rrr}
      {- 8} & {- 2} & {- 9} \\
      6 & 4 & 8 \\
      4 & 0 & 4
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ and $\text{w} = {\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      2 \\
      1 \\
      {- 2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,.}$ Determine if $\mathbf{w}$ is in Col $A$. Is $\mathbf{w}$ in Nul $A$?

Define $\left. T:\text{ℙ}_{2}\rightarrow\text{ℝ}^{2} \right.$ by $T\left( \textbf{p} \right) = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{l}
      {\textbf{p}(0)} \\
      {\textbf{p}(1)}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. For instance, if $\textbf{p}(t) = 3 + 5t + 7t^{2}$, then $T\left( \textbf{p} \right) = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      3 \\
      15
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$.

Define a linear transformation $\left. T:\text{ℙ}_{2}\rightarrow\text{ℝ}^{2} \right.$ by $T(\mathbf{p}) = \begin{bmatrix}
      {\mathbf{p}(0)} \\
      {\mathbf{p}(0)}
      \end{bmatrix}$. Find polynomials $\mathbf{p}_{1}$ and $\mathbf{p}_{2}$ in $\text{ℙ}_{2}$ that span the kernel of $T$, and describe the range of $T$.
